# Revealed-unit filtering implementation plan

## Objective

Restrict every SoulSearch window to units that DFHack considers revealed to the
player, including exclusion of ambushers/sneaking units and units on unrevealed
map tiles. Represent this mandatory rule as an implicit candidate filter that
participates in the normal provider pipeline without appearing in filter
management UI or user-owned filter state.

## Confirmed DFHack semantics

- `dfhack.units.isVisible(unit)` only checks whether the unit is on a visible
  map tile and explicitly does not account for sneaking.
- `dfhack.units.isHidden(unit)` is DFHack's combined "hidden to the player"
  predicate. It accounts for ambushing/sneaking, works in every game mode,
  falls back to map-tile visibility when appropriate, preserves DFHack's
  fortress-controlled-unit rules, and follows outer containers for caged
  units.
- The DFHack `spectate` plugin uses `not dfhack.units.isHidden(unit)` as its
  player-visible unit filter.
- SoulSearch will therefore define `is_revealed(unit)` as
  `not dfhack.units.isHidden(unit)`. Do not combine it with
  `dfhack.units.isVisible(unit)` unless live validation proves that SoulSearch
  intentionally needs stricter semantics than DFHack's own player-knowledge
  rule.

Source evidence in the adjacent DFHack 53.15-r1 checkout:

- `library/include/modules/Units.h`: `isVisible()` and `isHidden()` contracts.
- `library/modules/Units.cpp`: implementations of both predicates.
- `docs/dev/Lua API.rst`: Lua-facing behavior.
- `plugins/lua/spectate.lua`: existing Lua plugin usage.

## Architectural decisions

- Name the descriptor metadata `ui_hidden`, not `hidden`, to avoid ambiguity
  with `dfhack.units.isHidden()`.
- Keep `ui_hidden` and `implicit` as separate concepts:
  - `ui_hidden` controls presentation and user-management access.
  - the implicit-filter collection controls mandatory activation.
- Keep implicit filters separate from `FilterState`. User filters remain the
  only filters that are counted, edited, reset, saved in presets, or persisted
  in window settings.
- `SearchSession:get_candidate_filters()` may return user candidate filters plus
  implicit candidate filters. Existing public/user filter accessors must not
  expose implicit filters.
- Add a distinct `unit_visibility` candidate-filter family. Do not put the
  revealed descriptor in `unit_scope`: positive filters within one candidate
  family are OR-combined, while visibility must be AND-composed with unit-scope
  and race families.
- Compose providers in this order:
  `active -> unit visibility -> unit scope -> race -> resident snapshot`.
- Preserve the existing snapshot/refresh model. Automatically polling for
  units that become revealed while a window remains open is a separate feature.

## Non-goals

- Do not implement independent line-of-sight, viewport, announcement, or combat
  detection algorithms.
- Do not expose a user option for including hidden units.
- Do not persist the mandatory revealed-unit rule in presets or window settings.
- Do not reinterpret secret identities or curse concealment as map visibility.
- Do not change the OR-within-family / AND-between-providers candidate semantics.
- Do not add periodic candidate refreshes as part of this work.

## Phase 0 - Lock down contracts and current behavior

☐ Add characterization coverage for every current filter-management surface
  before changing descriptor presentation:
  - active filter choices;
  - attribute, skill, race, and unit-scope pickers;
  - the main-window active-filter count;
  - reset, preset save/load, and window-settings persistence.
☐ Add or extend `search_session_test.lua` coverage that distinguishes ranking
  filters, candidate filters, and the public ordered filter list.
☐ Add or extend candidate-pipeline characterization proving that unit-scope and
  race providers are AND-composed while positive filters within one family are
  OR-combined.
☐ Record the baseline local test and build results in the execution-evidence
  table before beginning Phase 1.

Exit gate: tests describe the current user-filter state, presentation, preset,
and candidate-composition boundaries without changing behavior.

## Phase 1 - Add hidden-filter presentation capability

☐ Extend `SoulSearchFilterDescriptor` with optional `ui_hidden: boolean` metadata;
  omitted/false must preserve all existing descriptor behavior.
☐ Add one shared descriptor predicate/helper for determining whether a filter
  is user-manageable. Do not duplicate raw `descriptor.ui_hidden` checks across
  unrelated UI owners.
☐ Update `filter_presenter` so hidden descriptors are omitted defensively from:
  - the active-filter list;
  - generic available-filter pickers;
  - skill choices and category headings;
  - race choices and group spacing;
  - unit-scope choices through the generic presenter.
☐ Ensure a result containing only hidden filters uses the same empty-state text
  as a result with no user filters.
☐ Make filter-management actions reject hidden descriptor IDs, even if invoked
  programmatically rather than through a picker.
☐ Count only user-manageable filters in the main-window `Filters: N` label.
☐ Add focused tests to `filter_presenter_test.lua`, `filter_state_test.lua`, and
  `filter_panel_test.lua` for hidden and ordinary descriptors together.

Exit gate: a hidden descriptor can exist in the catalog without appearing or
being mutable anywhere in the filter-management UI, and all ordinary filters
retain their current behavior.

## Phase 2 - Isolate and verify DFHack revealed-unit detection

☐ Add a small world-facing module, e.g. `unit_visibility.lua`, with a single
  `is_revealed(unit)` predicate implemented as
  `not dfhack.units.isHidden(unit)`.
☐ Keep DFHack access inside that module so provider tests do not need to know
  about raw unit flags or map designations.
☐ Add unit tests with a stubbed `dfhack.units.isHidden()` proving direct
  true/false delegation and one call per evaluated unit.
☐ Document why `dfhack.units.isVisible()` is insufficient and why SoulSearch
  delegates fortress-controlled and caged-unit semantics to DFHack.

Exit gate: revealed-unit semantics are represented by one tested predicate with
no candidate-pipeline or UI changes yet.

## Phase 3 - Add the mandatory implicit visibility filter

☐ Add `unit_visibility` to `SoulSearchFilterKind` and the filter constants.
☐ Add a unit-visibility descriptor catalog/group containing exactly one
  candidate descriptor:
  - id: `unit_visibility:revealed`;
  - label: `Revealed to player`;
  - behavior: `candidate`;
  - `ui_hidden=true`.
☐ Register the descriptor in the immutable global descriptor catalog and verify
  ID uniqueness, flat lookup, cache reset, and group membership.
☐ Add an immutable implicit-filter collection that selects
  `unit_visibility:revealed` in the positive/high direction for every search
  session.
☐ Keep implicit filters outside `FilterState`; expose a session accessor used
  only by candidate-pipeline construction.
☐ Make `SearchSession:get_candidate_filters()` return a fresh combined list of
  implicit and user candidate filters without allowing callers to mutate either
  source collection.
☐ Confirm the following operations affect only user filters and cannot remove,
  duplicate, serialize, or expose the implicit visibility filter:
  - clear/reset;
  - replace/load preset;
  - save preset;
  - window-settings update/reopen;
  - active-filter count and active-filter choice generation.
☐ Add session, filter-state, preset, settings, descriptor, and UI tests for
  these invariants.

Exit gate: every new search session owns the revealed-unit constraint, while
all existing user-facing filter APIs behave as if the implicit filter were not
part of editable state.

## Phase 4 - Compose visibility into candidate collection

☐ Add `unit_visibility_filter_provider.lua` following the existing candidate
  filter-family provider boundary and using `unit_visibility.is_revealed()` as
  its matcher.
☐ Insert the visibility provider immediately after `active_unit_provider` and
  before unit-scope and race providers in `SoulSearchWindow:refresh_candidates()`.
☐ Ensure the provider preserves upstream ordering, unit de-duplication, and
  upstream error propagation.
☐ Add focused provider tests covering:
  - revealed units included;
  - hidden units excluded;
  - hidden units excluded even when they match a positive unit scope;
  - hidden units excluded even when they match a positive race filter;
  - revealed units still subject to unit-scope exclusions and race filters;
  - malformed or absent user filters do not disable visibility enforcement.
☐ Extend `candidate_pipeline_characterization_test.lua` with the complete
  `active -> visibility -> unit scope -> race` composition.
☐ Extend main-window/UI characterization to prove initial collection, manual
  candidate refresh, filter changes, preset loads, and reset all retain the
  visibility provider.

Exit gate: no candidate can reach resident snapshot construction when DFHack
reports that unit as hidden, regardless of user filter state.

## Phase 5 - Automated validation and documentation

☐ Run the complete Lua test suite with `tools/Test.ps1` and record the exact
  test result.
☐ Run `tools/Build.ps1` and record the exact build result.
☐ Verify the built package contains every new production module and no test or
  planning artifacts outside the intended package shape.
☐ Update durable LuaDoc/comments for the implicit-filter and visibility-provider
  invariants; avoid phase-history narration in production code.
☐ Update `docs/project.todo` only after the automated exit gates are green;
  keep the item open if live proof remains outstanding.

Exit gate: all local automated checks are green and the packaged code matches
the source implementation.

## Phase 6 - Live DFHack verification

☐ Install/build the current checkout and run `soulsearch reload`; record any
  failure by its actual stack-trace owner rather than assuming SoulSearch caused
  it.
☐ Verify an ordinary revealed citizen, pet/livestock animal, visitor, wildlife
  unit, and hostile can appear when their user filters allow them.
☐ Verify a unit in an unrevealed map region does not appear.
☐ Verify an ambusher/sneaking unit does not appear before detection and can
  appear after DF reveals it and SoulSearch performs its normal candidate
  refresh/reopen flow.
☐ Verify a fortress-controlled and a caged unit behave consistently with the
  native UI and `dfhack.units.isHidden()`.
☐ Verify Reset filters, loading a preset, opening scoped Creatures-menu windows,
  and opening multiple SoulSearch windows never expose a hidden unit or display
  the implicit filter.
☐ Verify the active-filter count, active-filter list, every picker, and saved
  preset payload contain only user filters.
☐ Record the live commands, observations, and any unavailable scenario in the
  execution-evidence table.

Exit gate: live DFHack behavior demonstrates both stealth and unrevealed-map
exclusion without regressions to scoped windows or user filter management.

